    "cons": [
      "Costs more per tube than Alex Plus for jobs that do not move",
      "Not a wet-area or exterior high-movement sealant"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The tube to buy for baseboard, crown, casings and wainscot: it paints in half an hour and carries a real movement rating, which is what keeps trim joints from cracking every winter.",
    "description": "Alex Flex is DAP's trim-specific caulk, and it is the one we would put under a new coat of trim paint first. The difference from ordinary painter's caulk is on the spec sheet. DAP lists it as exceeding ASTM C920 Class 12.5, the elastomeric sealant standard, and a 2019 data sheet gives a dynamic joint movement of plus or minus 12.5% under ASTM C719. Ordinary acrylic latex is typically sold against the looser ASTM C834 latex sealant standard, which does not require that movement class. For trim, that matters at the joints that open and close with the seasons: the top of a baseboard on an exterior wall, the inside corners of crown, and the seam where a casing meets drywall. DAP says the sealant forms a skin dry enough to paint with latex or oil-based paint in 30 minutes, that it will not gloss or discolour paint, and it carries a 60-year durability claim. Water-ready time is 24 hours. Cleanup is soap and water."
  },
  {
    "id": "dap-alex-plus-18152",
    "name": "DAP Alex Plus Acrylic Latex Caulk Plus Silicone 18152, 10.1 oz",
    "category": "Siliconized Acrylic Latex, All-Purpose, ASTM C834",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
