    "bottomLine": "The default paintable caulk for low-movement work: nail-hole-free trim gaps, drywall corners, window casings and cabinet scribes. Step up to Alex Flex where joints move.",
    "description": "Alex Plus is the tube most painters already have in the truck, and for good reason. It is a siliconized acrylic latex caulk, which means acrylic latex with a silicone additive that DAP says gives greater flexibility and adhesion and less shrinkage than plain latex. DAP's product page says it is paintable within 30 minutes with latex or oil-based paint and gives it a 40-year durability rating, exceeding ASTM C834. One technical sheet lists a 24-hour water-ready time, and DAP notes that full cure depends on joint depth, temperature and humidity. For interior work where the joint does not move much, such as casings on interior walls, drywall-to-trim gaps, built-in cabinets and window stools, it is a sensible default that paints cleanly and tools easily. Where it falls short of Alex Flex is the movement rating: it is a C834 latex sealant, not a C920 Class 12.5 elastomeric, so on crown corners and exterior-wall baseboard it is more likely to show a hairline crack after a heating season."
  },
  {
    "id": "dap-alex-fast-dry-18425",
    "name": "DAP Alex Fast Dry Acrylic Latex Caulk Plus Silicone 18425, 10.1 oz",
    "category": "Siliconized Acrylic Latex, Fast Paint-Ready, Interior/Exterior",
    "badge": "Fastest to Paint",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "DAP directs a 20-minute skin time before brush or roller painting",
      "Retailers list it as spray-paintable immediately",
      "Low vertical sag listed at 1 mm on DAP's Canadian data sheet",
      "50-year durability on DAP's product sheet; interior and exterior use"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Faster skin means less time to tool long beads",
      "Same C834 class as Alex Plus, so it is not a moving-joint sealant"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it when caulk and paint have to happen on the same visit, such as a rental turnover or a one-day room repaint. For everything else, Alex Plus or Alex Flex is the better trade.",
    "description": "Alex Fast Dry exists for painters on a clock. DAP's directions say to allow 20 minutes for a skin to form before painting with a brush or roller, and retailer listings describe it as ready for spray painting immediately. That is ten minutes faster than Alex Plus on the brush side, which sounds trivial until you are caulking a room of trim and want to cut in the walls before lunch. It is still a siliconized acrylic latex caulk, it meets ASTM C834, and DAP's product sheet lists 50-year durability and interior and exterior use. DAP's Canadian data sheet gives a vertical sag of 1 mm, so it stays put in a vertical joint. The trade is working time. A caulk that skins fast gives you less time to tool a long bead before it starts to drag, so lay and tool in arm's-length sections. Like Alex Plus, it is sold against C834 rather than a C920 movement class, so it is not the pick for joints that move."
  },
  {
    "id": "sashco-big-stretch-10016",
    "name": "Sashco Big Stretch Acrylic Latex Caulk 10016, White, 10.5 oz",
    "category": "Elastomeric Acrylic Latex, Exterior and Interior, High Stretch",
    "badge": "Best for Exterior Trim",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Sashco rates it for roughly 500% elongation, far beyond ordinary latex",
      "Paintable with exterior latex after about 4 hours per Sashco's literature",
