    "bottomLine": "Choose it when a window or door joint needs silicone-grade weather resistance and also has to be painted. On ordinary interior trim, an acrylic is easier and cheaper.",
    "description": "Most silicone repels paint, which is why silicone is the wrong caulk for painted trim. GE's Supreme Paintable Silicone Window & Door is a silicone formulated to take paint, and it fills the gap for joints that need silicone's weather resistance but still have to match the trim colour. GE describes it as shrink and crack proof, which is the advantage over water-based caulk in a deep window joint. The paint time is where GE's own documents disagree. GE's current product listings give a 30-minute paint-ready time, while a GE data sheet for the same product family lists 60 minutes, so plan on the longer figure or check the label on the tube you buy. It is harder to work than acrylic: it strings, it does not wipe up with water, and a sloppy bead is harder to fix. If you are painting exterior window and door perimeters and want silicone, this is the one. Indoors on trim, Alex Flex is the better tool."
  },
  {
    "id": "white-lightning-painters-preferred-w13000010",
    "name": "White Lightning W13000010 Painter's Preferred Acrylic Latex Caulk, 10 oz",
    "category": "Acrylic Latex, Economy Painter's Caulk, Interior/Exterior",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Economy general-purpose acrylic latex, usually among the cheapest tubes on the shelf",
      "Paintable with water cleanup",
      "Rated for -30 to 180 degrees F performance",
      "Mould and mildew resistant once cured, per listings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Distributors list a 2 to 4 hour dry time, much slower to paint than Alex Fast Dry",
      "No published movement class or durability figure we could confirm",
      "Plain acrylic: expect more shrinkage and cracking in joints that move"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Fine for filling static gaps under paint on a big repaint where you are going through a case of tubes. For crown corners and exterior trim, spend more.",
    "description": "White Lightning Painter's Preferred is the honest budget pick: a general-purpose acrylic latex painter's caulk that is meant to be cheap and paintable, not high-performance. Distributor listings give a working time of about 5 to 7 minutes, a 2 to 4 hour dry time and a 7 to 14 day cure, with a -30 to 180 degree F performance range and mould and mildew resistance once cured. It cleans up with water and takes paint. What we could not find is a published movement class or durability figure, and that absence is informative. This is the kind of caulk that belongs in static joints: the gap between a stair stringer and the wall, a run of casing on an interior partition, the top of a backsplash you are painting. Use it on a whole-house repaint where you will go through a case of tubes and most joints do not move. Do not use it on crown corners, exterior-wall baseboard or anything outside that sees sun, where an Alex Flex or Big Stretch earns its higher price."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Paintable Caulk",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-paintable-caulk/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best paintable caulk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For interior trim, DAP Alex Flex Premium Molding and Trim Sealant. DAP lists a 30-minute paint-ready time and rates it to ASTM C920 Class 12.5, so it handles the seasonal movement at crown corners and exterior-wall baseboard better than ordinary painter's caulk. For exterior trim, Sashco Big Stretch if you can wait to paint, or Gorilla Ultimate Sealant Window and Door if you cannot."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there a paintable silicone caulk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
