          <p>
            The figures cluster at 30 minutes for a reason: that is what professional painters ask for, because it lets them caulk a room and cut in the same day. The outliers are informative. Alex Fast Dry trims ten minutes for crews on tight turnovers. Big Stretch is slow because a very stretchy acrylic needs more time before a paint film over it will hold, and Sashco is unusually candid that interior latex paint needs a day or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            All of these numbers assume a normal bead in mild, dry conditions. Gorilla is the only maker here that states its test conditions on the product page: a 3/16 inch bead at 70 degrees F and 50% humidity. A fat bead in a deep gap, a cold room in January or a humid bathroom in August will all push the time out. If a fingertip leaves a mark, it is not ready, whatever the tube says.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. If you need to paint the same day, buy one of the 30-minute products and lay thin beads. If you can wait overnight, paint-ready time stops mattering and movement rating becomes the figure to shop on. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">how long caulk takes to dry</a> covers cure times in more detail.
          </p>
