        <div id="shrinkage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Shrinkage: Why Trim Caulk Leaves a Dip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every water-based caulk shrinks as it dries, because part of what came out of the tube was water. On a hairline gap you will never see it. On a quarter-inch gap between a baseboard and a wavy wall, you will see a dip, sometimes a crack down the centre, and the paint will telegraph it exactly. This is the most common complaint about paintable caulk, and it is mostly a problem of using caulk for a gap it was not meant to fill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Siliconized acrylics such as Alex Plus are formulated to shrink less than plain acrylic, which is part of what you pay for over economy painter's caulk. GE describes its paintable silicone as shrink and crack proof, which is the main argument for it in deep window joints. None of the acrylic makers here publish a shrinkage percentage, so treat any number you see quoted online with suspicion. What you can rely on is the ordering: plain acrylic shrinks most, siliconized acrylic less, hybrids and silicones least.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fixes are mechanical, not chemical. On gaps wider than about a quarter inch, or deeper than they are wide, push in a foam backer rod first so the caulk is a shallow bead rather than a deep plug. On wide interior gaps, plan for two passes: fill, let it shrink overnight, then lay a thin finishing bead before painting. And stop asking caulk to fill holes. Nail holes, splits and dents in trim want a filler that sands; our guides to the <a href="/best-wood-filler/">best wood filler</a> and <a href="/best-spackle/">best spackle</a> cover those.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more shrinkage trap catches people on crown moulding. A bead that looks perfect when wet can pull back from one face as it dries, leaving a hairline line of shadow along the ceiling. That is usually a sign the joint was too wide for one pass or the bead was tooled too thin at the edges. A slightly fuller bead tooled with a damp finger, and a second pass where needed, is the cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="movement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Movement Ratings: C834 vs C920
          </h2>

          <p>
            If paint-ready time tells you when you can paint, the movement rating tells you whether the paint will still look right next spring. It is the most useful number on a caulk tube and the one fewest buyers look for. Two standards show up on the paintable caulks here.
          </p>

          <p>
            ASTM C834 is the standard for latex sealants. Alex Plus and Alex Fast Dry are sold as meeting or exceeding it, and it is the normal specification for painter's caulk. It does not carry the movement classes of the elastomeric standard. In practice, a C834 caulk is designed for joints that move only a little.
          </p>

          <p>
            ASTM C920 is the standard for elastomeric joint sealants, and it comes with movement classes such as 12.5, 25 and higher, describing the percentage of joint movement the cured sealant is rated to handle. DAP lists Alex Flex as exceeding C920 Class 12.5, and a DAP data sheet gives a dynamic movement figure of plus or minus 12.5%. That is the single biggest technical reason it beats Alex Plus on trim: it is rated for the seasonal open-and-close of a baseboard on an exterior wall or the inside corners of crown.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for the joint that moves most.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a room has crown moulding or baseboard on exterior walls, caulk the whole room with a C920-rated trim caulk such as Alex Flex. Saving a few dollars on C834 caulk for the static joints and switching tubes mid-room is not worth the hairline cracks you will repaint next year.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Outside, movement goes up by an order of magnitude because sun heats a dark fascia board far more than any interior trim. That is why Sashco publishes elongation for Big Stretch rather than a modest movement class, and why hybrids like Gorilla Ultimate are the better tools for exterior casings. For the high-movement exterior joints on siding and masonry, our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> guide ranks the dedicated elastomeric and hybrid sealants.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint-compat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Painting Over It: What Sticks and What Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paintable means paint will adhere. It does not mean every paint will look good on every caulk, and there are three combinations that cause most of the trouble.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stiff paint over stretchy caulk</h3>

          <p>
            A very flexible bead under a brittle paint film is a mismatch. Sashco says so directly: some low-end flat latex paints may crack over Big Stretch, regardless of cure time. The paint cannot follow the bead as it moves. The fix is a quality acrylic paint, ideally the same premium exterior paint you are using on the trim, and the full paint wait. The same principle applies, more mildly, to hybrids.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil-based paint too soon</h3>

          <p>
            DAP rates Alex Plus and Alex Flex for latex or oil-based paint at 30 minutes, but slower caulks are a different story. Sashco lists about a week before most oil-based paints or stains go over Big Stretch. If you are still using oil or alkyd enamel on trim, check the caulk's oil-paint time specifically, not just its latex figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sheen and colour flashing</h3>

          <p>
            On semi-gloss and gloss trim, caulk can show as a duller or shinier line because it absorbs paint differently from the primed wood next to it. DAP claims Alex Flex will not gloss or discolour paint, which is a point in its favour on high-sheen work. A second finish coat over the whole trim, rather than spot-painting the caulk line, hides the difference best. For trim paint and primer picks, see our guides to the <a href="/best-interior-paint/">best interior paint</a> and <a href="/best-primer/">best primer</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paintable silicone deserves its own caution. It accepts paint far better than standard silicone, but it is still silicone underneath, and the safest approach is to follow GE's label for paint type and timing and to test a short section before painting a whole window. Standard silicone, whatever the tube says about versatility, should never be used where paint has to go on top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="interior-exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Interior Trim vs Exterior Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest decision on this page is whether the joint is inside or outside, because the demands are completely different and very few tubes are the best choice for both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interior trim</h3>

          <p>
            Inside, joints move a little with humidity and the house settling, they never see rain or ultraviolet light, and they have to disappear under semi-gloss paint. You want a caulk that tools smoothly, paints in 30 minutes, does not shrink into a visible dip and carries a movement rating for the corners that do open up. Alex Flex is the best fit, Alex Plus is the value pick for static joints, and Alex Fast Dry is the choice when the paint goes on the same visit. Economy painter's caulk like White Lightning is acceptable for large repaints of static joints. Hybrids and paintable silicone are more sealant than interior trim needs and harder to tool cleanly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exterior trim</h3>

          <p>
            Outside, the joints between trim boards, corner boards, fascia and siding move with every sunny afternoon, see rain and need a bead that survives years of UV. A C834 interior caulk will usually crack there within a season or two. Big Stretch is the paintable acrylic for this work if you can wait the paint time; Gorilla Ultimate is the hybrid when you need to paint the same day; and GE's paintable silicone is the choice when a window or door perimeter needs silicone-grade weathering but must match the trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            For exterior jobs where paint is optional, and especially for siding, windows and masonry, our dedicated guides go further: the <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> rankings, plus the <a href="/best-caulk-for-wood-siding/">best caulk for wood siding</a> and <a href="/best-caulk-for-windows/">best caulk for windows</a>. This page stays with the paintable products that bridge both sides of the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            One rule covers the grey areas such as a porch ceiling, a garage door casing or a sunroom. If it can get wet or sees direct sun, treat it as exterior.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint-grade-bead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Getting a Paint-Grade Bead
          </h2>

          <p>
            The difference between trim that looks professionally painted and trim that looks caulked is almost entirely in the bead, not the brand. A few habits matter more than any product choice on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle small. For trim, a 1/8 inch opening is enough for most joints, and you can always cut more. A big opening lays too much caulk, which you then smear across the trim and wall, and the smear shows through paint as a ridge. A smooth, dripless <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">caulk gun</a> helps more than people expect, because a gun that keeps pushing after you release the trigger is how blobs happen at the end of a run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tool once, with a damp finger or a profiled tool, and then leave it. Going back over a bead that has started to skin, especially with Alex Fast Dry, drags it and leaves a textured line that paint will not hide. On long runs, work in arm's-length sections. Wipe the excess on a damp rag as you go rather than letting it dry on the face of the trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tape only where you need a crisp line. On stained-and-painted combinations, or where trim meets a wall colour you are not repainting, a line of <a href="/best-painters-tape/">painter's tape</a> on each side gives a sharp bead edge. Pull the tape while the caulk is still wet. On painted-both-sides joints, tape is usually more trouble than it is worth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, caulk after priming and before the finish coats. Caulk bonds better to primed wood than bare, and the finish coats then cover caulk and trim as one surface, which hides the sheen difference described above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where Paintable Caulk Is the Wrong Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paintable caulk is the right answer on trim. It is the wrong answer in several places people routinely use it, and knowing them saves a lot of repeat work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tubs, showers and anything that stays wet. Siliconized acrylic resists water once cured, but it is not a shower sealant, and it does not hold up to standing water and daily soaping the way a 100% silicone does. Painted bathroom trim is fine; the tub-to-tile joint is not. Our <a href="/best-bathroom-caulk/">best bathroom caulk</a> guide covers what belongs there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gaps that should stay open. Window weep holes, the bottom edge of lap siding and the drainage gaps at the base of some window and door frames exist to let water out. Caulking them with a paintable product so the trim looks tidy traps water behind it. Our guide on <a href="/where-not-to-caulk/">where not to caulk</a> lists the joints to leave alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wide structural gaps and holes. Caulk is a flexible seal for a joint, not a filler for a hole. Gaps over half an inch, split trim, missing chunks and nail holes want backer rod, wood filler, spackle or a replacement piece. Paintable caulk in a big void will shrink, crack and show through the paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joints that move a lot outside. An interior painter's caulk on a sunny fascia joint will crack within a season. That is not a defect in the caulk; it is the wrong caulk. Use Big Stretch, a hybrid or a dedicated exterior sealant, and if cracking keeps coming back, our guide to <a href="/why-does-exterior-caulk-keep-cracking/">why exterior caulk keeps cracking</a> works through the causes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recaulking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Recaulking Painted Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            Much of the paintable caulk sold every year goes on top of old, cracked paintable caulk, and that is where a lot of trim jobs go wrong. New caulk over a cracked bead inherits the crack, and the paint telegraphs it within months.
          </p>

          <p>
            On interior trim, the old caulk is almost always latex, and the clean way to deal with it is to soften and remove the failed bead rather than caulking over the gap. A latex remover gel, a utility knife and a plastic scraper are enough. Our sister guide to the <a href="/best-caulk-remover/">best caulk remover</a> covers which products suit painted trim, and why some of the strongest removers are not recommended on painted surfaces. Expect to touch up paint along the edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions. A sound latex bead with a hairline crack along one edge can sometimes be cleaned, primed and given a thin new bead of the same type. Our answer to <a href="/can-you-caulk-over-caulk/">can you caulk over caulk</a> explains when that works and when it does not. Silicone is the exception that never works: no paintable caulk will bond to it, so any old silicone on painted trim has to come out completely, along with its residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the new bead goes on, use the product the joint actually needs rather than whatever is in the drawer. If the old caulk cracked at a crown corner or an exterior-wall baseboard, that is a joint that moves, and replacing a C834 painter's caulk with another C834 painter's caulk is likely to produce the same crack next winter. That is the case where Alex Flex, with its C920 Class 12.5 rating, earns its higher price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over caulk that is only paint-ready by the clock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint-ready times assume a thin bead in mild, dry conditions. A deep bead, a cold room or high humidity all slow the skin. Painting over soft caulk drags it and leaves a ridge that shows through every coat. If a fingertip leaves a mark, wait longer, whatever the tube says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using painter's caulk on joints that move</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plain and siliconized acrylics sold against ASTM C834 are designed for joints that barely move. On crown corners, exterior-wall baseboards and outdoor trim they crack within a season. Buy a C920-rated trim caulk such as Alex Flex indoors, and Big Stretch or a hybrid outside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling wide gaps in one deep pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deep plug of water-based caulk shrinks into a visible dip and often cracks down the centre. On gaps wider than about a quarter inch, push in foam backer rod first, keep the bead shallow, and plan for a second thin pass after the first has shrunk overnight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking over silicone and expecting paint to stick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No paintable caulk bonds to old silicone, and paint does not stick to standard silicone either. If the old bead on your trim is silicone, remove it completely and clean off the residue before you lay a paintable caulk, or the new bead will peel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using cheap flat paint over stretchy caulk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sashco warns that some low-end flat latex paints may crack over Big Stretch, because a stiff paint film cannot follow a bead that stretches that much. Use a quality acrylic paint over elastomeric caulk and hybrids, and give the caulk its full paint wait before the first coat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best paintable caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For interior trim, DAP Alex Flex Premium Molding and Trim Sealant. DAP lists a 30-minute paint-ready time and rates it to ASTM C920 Class 12.5, so it handles the seasonal movement at crown corners and exterior-wall baseboard better than ordinary painter's caulk. For exterior trim, Sashco Big Stretch if you can wait to paint, or Gorilla Ultimate Sealant Window and Door if you cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a paintable silicone caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. GE Supreme Paintable Silicone Window and Door is formulated to accept paint, and GE describes it as shrink and crack proof. GE's own sources give 30 minutes on its product page and 60 minutes on a data sheet for paint-ready time, so plan on the longer figure. Standard 100% silicone does not accept paint and should not be used on painted trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What caulk is best for painting trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A siliconized acrylic latex made for trim. DAP Alex Flex is the best fit because it combines a 30-minute paint time with a C920 movement rating. DAP Alex Plus is the value choice for joints that barely move, and Alex Fast Dry suits jobs where you caulk and paint on the same visit. Economy painter's caulk is fine only on static joints.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can you paint over paintable caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product. DAP Alex Fast Dry wants 20 minutes before brushing; Alex Plus, Alex Flex and Gorilla Ultimate list 30 minutes. GE's paintable silicone is listed at 30 to 60 minutes. Sashco Big Stretch needs about 4 hours for exterior latex, 1 to 2 days for interior latex and about a week for oil-based paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my paintable caulk crack after painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because the joint moves more than the caulk is rated for, the bead was laid too deep and shrank, or the paint went on before the caulk had skinned properly. A stiff, cheap flat paint over a stretchy caulk can also crack. Match the caulk's movement rating to the joint, use backer rod on deep gaps and wait the full paint time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use paintable caulk outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some of it. Alex Plus and Alex Fast Dry are rated for interior and exterior use, but they are C834 latex caulks best kept to low-movement exterior joints. For trim boards, fascia and casings that see sun and movement, Sashco Big Stretch or a paintable hybrid such as Gorilla Ultimate is the better choice, and siding and masonry belong to dedicated exterior sealants.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is acrylic latex or siliconized acrylic better for trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Siliconized acrylic, for most trim. The silicone additive improves adhesion and flexibility, and DAP says it reduces shrinkage compared with plain latex. Plain acrylic painter's caulk is cheaper and fine for static gaps on a big repaint, but it is the type most likely to show a dip or crack under semi-gloss paint at joints that move.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
